WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.901
Construction payment risk often becomes visible only after

2
00:00:05.941 --> 00:00:07.255
delays begin to escalate.

3
00:00:07.295 --> 00:00:09.800
I built this system to identify developing risk

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.941
earlier, so operational teams can prioritize the workflows

5
00:00:12.981 --> 00:00:14.349
that need attention first.

6
00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:17.538
The engineering problem is not to make

7
00:00:17.578 --> 00:00:18.405
a legal decision.

8
00:00:18.445 --> 00:00:20.752
It is to rank operational workflows by modeled

9
00:00:20.792 --> 00:00:22.895
escalation risk, while there is still time

10
00:00:22.935 --> 00:00:25.038
for a person to investigate and intervene.

11
00:00:26.328 --> 00:00:28.993
All development and evaluation data is synthetic.

12
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.698
The feature pipeline captures operational signals

13
00:00:31.738 --> 00:00:33.575
such as prior escalation behavior,

14
00:00:33.615 --> 00:00:36.722
payment-chain completeness, critical missing information,

15
00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:38.488
and conflicting project details.

16
00:00:39.778 --> 00:00:41.994
The data layer is built around Amazon S3,

17
00:00:42.034 --> 00:00:43.149
AWS Glue, and Athena.

18
00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:46.285
This creates a governed analytical pipeline that supports

19
00:00:46.325 --> 00:00:49.696
both machine-learning workflows and the operational dashboard.

20
00:00:50.986 --> 00:00:53.519
Instead of relying on a random train-test split,

21
00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:54.966
I used temporal validation.

22
00:00:55.006 --> 00:00:58.717
Development, validation, and untouched out-of-time test periods remain

23
00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:01.718
ordered, reducing the risk that future workflow patterns

24
00:01:01.758 --> 00:01:02.737
leak into training.

25
00:01:04.027 --> 00:01:07.640
Logistic Regression v1 remains the production champion,

26
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.094
with an operational review threshold frozen at 0.20.

27
00:01:11.134 --> 00:01:14.349
I also ran a separate DataRobot AutoML challenger

28
00:01:14.389 --> 00:01:17.936
study. Elastic-Net achieved a verified holdout ROC-AUC

29
00:01:17.976 --> 00:01:21.921
of 0.684, but the studies use different temporal partitions.

30
00:01:23.211 --> 00:01:25.598
A small leaderboard improvement is not enough

31
00:01:25.638 --> 00:01:27.702
to replace a governed production model.

32
00:01:27.742 --> 00:01:29.914
Promotion requires comparable validation,

33
00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:31.532
meaningful repeatable benefit,

34
00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:34.067
operational compatibility, and explicit review.

35
00:01:35.357 --> 00:01:38.329
Every prediction is designed for human interpretation.

36
00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:41.342
The production model exposes readable feature drivers,

37
00:01:41.382 --> 00:01:44.019
with SHAP used as a secondary analytical method.

38
00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:47.199
These explain statistical associations in synthetic data,

39
00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:48.984
not causal or legal conclusions.

40
00:01:50.274 --> 00:01:52.422
The final production path runs on AWS.

41
00:01:52.462 --> 00:01:55.820
Users reach a Streamlit operational dashboard through HTTPS

42
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.375
and a public load balancer.

43
00:01:57.415 --> 00:02:01.233
The dashboard accesses Athena-backed workflow data and communicates

44
00:02:01.273 --> 00:02:04.976
with an internal FastAPI prediction service running independently

45
00:02:05.016 --> 00:02:06.070
on ECS and Fargate.

46
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.289
The model is exposed through a containerized FastAPI

47
00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:11.830
service with health checks,

48
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.714
model metadata, typed validation,

49
00:02:13.754 --> 00:02:16.168
versioned contracts, and automated testing.

50
00:02:17.458 --> 00:02:20.295
Production monitoring covers application health,

51
00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:22.393
prediction behavior, input quality,

52
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.472
and drift signals.

53
00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:24.971
CloudWatch logs, metrics,

54
00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.649
alarms, documented runbooks,

55
00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:30.366
and audit records make the system observable after deployment.

56
00:02:31.656 --> 00:02:35.057
The system deliberately stops before consequential judgment.

57
00:02:35.097 --> 00:02:37.465
AI produces a risk signal and explanation.

58
00:02:37.505 --> 00:02:39.817
A person reviews the evidence and decides

59
00:02:39.857 --> 00:02:41.193
what operational action,

60
00:02:41.233 --> 00:02:42.455
if any, should follow.

61
00:02:43.745 --> 00:02:47.102
The result is an end-to-end machine-learning product spanning

62
00:02:47.142 --> 00:02:49.051
data engineering, model governance,

63
00:02:49.091 --> 00:02:51.446
explainability, APIs, cloud infrastructure,

64
00:02:51.486 --> 00:02:53.952
monitoring, and human-in-the-loop operations.

65
00:02:55.242 --> 00:02:57.574
This is Construction Payment Risk Prediction.

66
00:02:57.614 --> 00:02:59.208
Designed not simply as a model,

67
00:02:59.248 --> 00:03:01.421
but as a production AI engineering system.

